# =============================================================================
# FAULTMAVEN — STANDALONE CONFIGURATION
# =============================================================================
# You only MUST set ONE thing: an LLM provider's API key (step 1 below).
# Everything else has a working default and can stay untouched.
#
#   1. cp .env.example .env
#   2. pick a provider + set its API key (section 1)
#   3. ./faultmaven.sh start        →    http://localhost:3333
#
# HOW DEFAULTS WORK
#   - Commented `# KEY=value` lines below show the DEFAULT that the app already
#     uses (from settings.py). Leave them commented to accept the default;
#     uncomment + change a line only to override it.
#   - settings.py is the source of truth for defaults; this file mirrors it, and
#     scripts/check_env_example_sync.py fails CI if the two ever drift.
#   - The JWT signing secret is auto-generated and persisted (data/.jwt_secret)
#     on first run — you do NOT need to set it.
#
# This file targets a STANDALONE install (single process, single user: SQLite,
# in-process cache, embedded ChromaDB, local files, local auth) — that is what
# the defaults are tuned for, and sections 1–4 are all a standalone run needs.
#
# Sections 5–7 also document knobs that only a CLOUD deployment exercises (the
# RS256 key pair, the OAuth flow, Opik tracing). They
# are listed so this file mirrors the full settings.py surface key-for-key and
# nothing drifts unnoticed; in a real cloud deployment those values come from
# Kubernetes ConfigMaps/Secrets (ADR-004), NOT from this file. Standalone users
# can leave every one of them commented.
#
# FaultMaven Cloud users don't need this file at all: point the extension at
# https://app.faultmaven.ai
#
# Running standalone on a remote box? Keep localhost and tunnel:
#   ssh -L 3333:localhost:3333 -L 8090:localhost:8090 user@server
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. LLM PROVIDER  (REQUIRED — the one thing you must set)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED: set the credential for your provider. CHAT_PROVIDER itself has a
# shipped default (below), but no credential ships with it — the server refuses
# to start when the resolved provider's API key (or LOCAL_LLM_URL for 'local')
# is missing, so a default provider is never silently used without a key.
# Cloud providers need a key; 'local' (Ollama/vLLM) needs a URL instead.
# Options: openai | anthropic | gemini | fireworks | groq | huggingface |
#          cohere | openrouter | local
# The value below is the RECOMMENDED provider, and every default in this file
# is tuned for it. Uncomment as-is, or change it to your provider:
# CHAT_PROVIDER=gemini
#
# MODEL REQUIREMENTS (learned over time — getting these wrong is the usual cause
# of poor results; check them before pinning a non-default model for CHAT):
#
# 1) SCHEMA ENFORCEMENT. FaultMaven drives the engine with large schema-
#    constrained responses, so CHAT_PROVIDER must ENFORCE the schema natively
#    (tool calling or json_schema response_format). A provider that only
#    requests it in-prompt lets the model omit required fields → the engine
#    drops state → empty/degraded investigations.
#      Enforced (good for CHAT): openai, anthropic, gemini (1.5+, incl.
#        2.5/3.x), openrouter routed to an openai/* model, groq gpt-oss.
#      Best-effort only (fine for cheap CLASSIFIER/SYNTHESIS overrides, NOT for
#        primary CHAT): fireworks (incl. deepseek-v3/minimax), groq
#        (llama/mixtral), cohere, huggingface, local.
# 2) TOOL CALLING. The investigation agent calls tools (search_file, KB,
#    deep_analysis); a model without function/tool calling loses them and
#    degrades badly. HuggingFace's Inference API has none — don't use it for CHAT.
# 3) CONTEXT-WINDOW HEADROOM. One turn runs a multi-step tool loop whose
#    accumulated context grows well past the flat prompt budget
#    (PROMPT_TARGET_TOKENS, default 32K — section 2). The window must leave room
#    for that loop + the model's reasoning + its output, so prefer a generous
#    one (~320K+ is comfortable). The curated defaults span 200K (Claude) to 1M
#    (Gemini / GPT-4.1 / GPT-5); Claude's 200K is the tightest but still ~6× the
#    budget — fine for typical cases, reach for a 1M-window model on very deep
#    ones. The 32K budget does NOT scale with the window: the window is
#    headroom, not fill.
# 4) THINKING UNDER CONTROL. "Thinking"/reasoning models bill hidden reasoning
#    against the output budget and can starve the response into a truncated or
#    empty result. FaultMaven caps this automatically for Gemini 3.x; if you pin
#    another reasoning model (o1/o3, …) watch for empty/truncated turns.

# --- Set the API key for the provider you chose (uncomment ONE) ---
# OPENAI_API_KEY=
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# GEMINI_API_KEY=
# FIREWORKS_API_KEY=
# GROQ_API_KEY=
# HUGGINGFACE_API_KEY=
# COHERE_API_KEY=
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# --- Local LLM (no API key; set CHAT_PROVIDER=local) ---
# LOCAL_LLM_URL=http://host.docker.internal:11434   # Linux: http://172.17.0.1:11434
# LOCAL_LLM_MODEL=llama3.2
#   If you use a local model you MUST set PROMPT_TARGET_TOKENS below to fit its
#   context window. Note: Ollama defaults num_ctx to ~2048-8192 regardless of
#   the model's nominal size — so set e.g. PROMPT_TARGET_TOKENS=8000 (or raise
#   num_ctx on the Ollama side). Same applies to any provider/model whose
#   context window is below the 32000 default.

# --- BASE model per provider (also the CHAT/investigation model) ---
# The commented values ARE the built-in defaults (CI-synced to settings.py):
# leave them alone unless you want a different model. Not one-model-per-provider:
# any role can pin its own {PROVIDER}_{ROLE}_MODEL (see "Per-ROLE routing"
# below) and run a different model of the same provider alongside this one.
# Defaults favor performance (token-usage billing → quality = your experience).
# IMPORTANT: every default model shown below has a large context window (200K–1M,
# see requirement 3 above), so the default PROMPT_TARGET_TOKENS=32000 (section 2)
# fits all of them with headroom and no extra config. If you pin a DIFFERENT
# model here — or use a provider with no
# default below (local, huggingface) — you are responsible for setting
# PROMPT_TARGET_TOKENS to match THAT model's context window. Smaller model →
# lower it (e.g. 8000); larger/advanced model → you may raise it.
# OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-5.6-luna
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-sonnet-4-6
# GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-3.7-flash
#   gemini-3.5-flash and gemini-3.5-flash-lite are also supported; the adapter
#   applies each generation's API surface automatically by model version
#   (docs/reference/llm-model-capabilities.md).
#   gemini-3.7-flash is also supported (native vision, tool calling, STRICT
#   structured output). It runs Google's reduced 3.7 API surface — no
#   temperature/topP/topK, thinkingLevel-only reasoning control, and the
#   3.6+ tool-result shape (user-role function responses carrying the call
#   id) — which FaultMaven's adapter applies automatically by model version;
#   gemini-3.5-* requests are unaffected. FaultMaven pins 3.7 thinking to
#   the lowest level on every call (thinking tokens bill at the full OUTPUT
#   rate). Introductory pricing $0.75/$3.75 per 1M in/out runs through
#   2026-12-31 (then $1.50/$7.50 — see infrastructure/llm/pricing.py).
# Fireworks is BEST-EFFORT for structured output (no native schema enforcement)
# — see requirement 1 above; prefer an enforced provider for CHAT_PROVIDER.
# FIREWORKS_MODEL=accounts/fireworks/models/deepseek-v4-flash
# GROQ_MODEL=llama-3.3-70b-versatile
# COHERE_MODEL=command-r-plus
# OPENROUTER_MODEL=anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-6
# HuggingFace works but is NOT recommended: its Inference API does not support
# tool calling, so investigation quality is degraded (no search_file, etc.).
# HUGGINGFACE_MODEL=mistralai/Mistral-Large-Instruct-2411

# --- Per-ROLE routing: a (provider, model) pair per LLM role ---
# Every LLM-calling role can be routed independently. A role PROVIDER left
# UNSET follows CHAT_PROVIDER; a role PROVIDER that is set is a STATIC
# assignment — it stays put when CHAT_PROVIDER changes, and needs its own API
# key. The role's MODEL comes from that provider's {PROVIDER}_{ROLE}_MODEL,
# else its base {PROVIDER}_MODEL — so two roles on the SAME provider can run
# different models.
# Roles: da (investigation tool loop), classifier, synthesis (QA sub-agent),
# knowledge (doc→runbook conversion), multimodal, code,
# structured_output (schema-bound engine calls; see requirement 1).
# NOTE: schema-enforcement class is checked per resolved role at startup —
# best-effort models are fine for classifier/synthesis, and WARN for
# investigation/chat.
#
# SHIPPED DEFAULTS (the values below are what runs if you change nothing):
#   classifier, synthesis, multimodal -> pinned to gemini. They stay on Gemini
#     when you flip CHAT_PROVIDER, which is what makes an A/B comparison of the
#     anchor a controlled one. Change them, or comment them out to follow
#     CHAT_PROVIDER, if that is not what you want.
#   da, knowledge, structured_output  -> unset, so they follow CHAT_PROVIDER.
# CLASSIFIER_PROVIDER=gemini
# SYNTHESIS_PROVIDER=gemini
# MULTIMODAL_PROVIDER=gemini
# DA_PROVIDER=
# KNOWLEDGE_PROVIDER=
# STRUCTURED_OUTPUT_PROVIDER=
# Per-role models (any {PROVIDER}_{ROLE}_MODEL works). The two Gemini values
# below are shipped defaults: the cheap lite tier for the two small-output
# roles, while chat/investigation stays on GEMINI_MODEL above.
# GEMINI_CLASSIFIER_MODEL=gemini-3.5-flash-lite
# GEMINI_SYNTHESIS_MODEL=gemini-3.5-flash-lite
# OPENAI_CLASSIFIER_MODEL=
# OPENAI_DA_MODEL=   <- needs DA_PROVIDER set as well; see the note below
#
# DA is the ONE exception to the model-resolution rule above. The investigation
# tool loop passes a per-role model only when a dedicated DA provider exists, so
# {PROVIDER}_DA_MODEL on its own never reaches the wire — the base
# {PROVIDER}_MODEL runs instead, silently. To pin a different investigation
# model on the SAME provider, set DA_PROVIDER to that provider as well (it may
# name the same one as CHAT_PROVIDER). Every other role passes its per-role
# model unconditionally, so classifier/synthesis/knowledge need no such pairing.

# OpenAI reasoning effort (none|low|medium|high) — operator DEFAULT for
# reasoning models' hidden thinking. UNSET by default: the shape-based policy
# applies ("none" for plain chat on families verified to accept it, a "low"
# floor on structured calls so hidden reasoning can't starve the schema body).
# When set, it replaces that shape default; per-call declared intent and hard
# model constraints still win. Values that would predictably truncate output
# degrade toward LESS reasoning with a warning ("medium"/"high" on structured
# calls clamp to "low"; "none" falls to "low" where unverified). See
# ANTHROPIC_THINKING_MODE below for the Anthropic analog.
# OPENAI_REASONING_EFFORT=

# Anthropic extended thinking on tool-calling (structured-output) requests
# (#1116). OFF by default — "off" sends no thinking parameter at all.
# "adaptive" is the mechanism on Claude 4.6+ (the model decides how much to
# think); "enabled" uses a fixed budget_tokens and applies to pre-4.6 models
# only (budget_tokens is deprecated on 4.6 and rejected with a 400 on 4.7+).
# Thinking is billed INSIDE max_tokens; a budget that would starve the visible
# answer is downgraded to no-thinking with a warning instead of being sent.
# ANTHROPIC_THINKING_MODE=off
# ANTHROPIC_THINKING_BUDGET_TOKENS=4096

# Tool calling (requirement 2) is enforced at startup: if your investigation
# model (DA_PROVIDER, else CHAT_PROVIDER) can't do tool calling, the server
# REFUSES TO START — the engine needs tools to gather evidence, and concluding
# without them is unsafe. To run degraded/offline anyway (e.g. a local model
# with no tools: no search_file/deep_analysis, structural-index summaries only),
# opt in explicitly. The usual fix is to use a tool-capable CHAT_PROVIDER
# (anthropic, openai, gemini) — or set DA_PROVIDER to override just the
# investigation provider while keeping a different CHAT_PROVIDER.
# ALLOW_TOOLLESS_INVESTIGATION=false

# Strict provider mode — on by default: a failed call fails the turn instead of
# silently retrying on a different provider, so one investigation is the product
# of one model. Set false to allow the fallback chain, accepting that a turn may
# be answered by a provider you did not choose:
# STRICT_PROVIDER_MODE=true

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. OPTIONAL  (defaults shown; uncomment to change)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Prompt budget (tokens) — the whole assembled prompt is budget-allocated to
# this flat target, independent of the model's context window. 32000 is safe
# for the curated cloud models above (200K–1M context); the window is headroom
# for the tool loop + reasoning + output, not extra prompt fill. FaultMaven
# clamps this down automatically for models it knows are smaller; for models it
# does not know (local/custom), it trusts this value — so set it yourself to fit:
#   - LOCAL models: set to your model's usable context, e.g. 8000. Ollama
#     defaults num_ctx to ~2048-8192 unless you raise it, so the prompt will be
#     truncated if this is left at 32000.
#   - Advanced/large models: raise it (e.g. 64000) to use more context — note
#     this increases per-turn cost linearly.
# PROMPT_TARGET_TOKENS=32000

# Per-turn spend control (sum across all tool-loop calls in a turn). Both guards
# compare a COST-WEIGHTED spend — `spend_weighted_tokens = input + output +
# cache_write + 0.25*cache_read` (cache reads are billed at a fraction, so they
# are down-weighted) — not raw tokens. The per-call jar above still bounds each
# individual call.
#   - PROMPT_TURN_TOKEN_CEILING (default 150000): HARD safety abort — once a
#     turn's cost-weighted spend crosses it, the tool loop wraps up schema-only
#     on the next iteration instead of making more calls.
#   - PROMPT_TURN_TOKEN_BUDGET (default 100000): SOFT budget — when crossed, an
#     end-of-turn WARNING (turn_token_budget_exceeded) is logged. Observability
#     only, no behavior change. Set 0 to disable the alert.
#   - PROMPT_CONVERSATION_HISTORY_MAX_TOKENS (default 8000): cap on the
#     conversation-history section so verbose old turns can't starve the rest.
#   - PROMPT_TOOL_OBSERVATION_MAX_TOKENS (default 16000): bounded scratchpad for
#     accumulated tool-loop observations; the oldest are elided (with a marker)
#     when exceeded.
# PROMPT_TURN_TOKEN_CEILING=150000
# PROMPT_TURN_TOKEN_BUDGET=100000
# PROMPT_CONVERSATION_HISTORY_MAX_TOKENS=8000
# PROMPT_TOOL_OBSERVATION_MAX_TOKENS=16000

# LLM cost estimates — the per-turn `turn_token_spend` log and the
# `llm_cost_usd_total` metric price token usage from a built-in table of
# best-effort provider rates. Override any rate (e.g. after a provider price
# change, or for a self-hosted/custom model) with a JSON object of USD-per-1M
# rates; unset entries fall back to the defaults. Unknown models are reported
# UNPRICED rather than guessed. See
# docs/operations/monitoring/llm-cost-observability.md.
# LLM_PRICING_OVERRIDES={"anthropic":{"claude-sonnet-4-6":{"input":3.0,"output":15.0,"cache_read":0.30,"cache_write":3.75}}}

# Web search — enabled by default, but only works once a Tavily key is set:
# ENABLE_WEB_SEARCH=true
# TAVILY_API_KEY=

# KB cause seeder — off by default (fm#1295: measured not to help diagnosis).
# Seeds a matched runbook's causal chain as CANDIDATE hypotheses at the
# symptom-verified transition. Runbooks still reach the model as prose and via
# the KB QA tools with it off. Set true only for a measurement run:
# FAULTMAVEN_KB_CAUSE_SEEDER=false

# Chain-authored root-cause conclusion — on by default. A standing validated,
# uncontested causal-chain root outranks the assistant's own free-text
# conclusion: the surfaced root cause is rendered from the chain, so it cannot
# assert more than the evidence proves, and the assistant's conclusion is used
# only when no such root stands. Set false as the kill switch to let the
# assistant's conclusion win whenever it wrote one:
# FAULTMAVEN_CHAIN_AUTHORED_CONCLUSION=true

# KB orphan repair (advanced) — at startup the KB bootstrap re-embeds any
# knowledge_items row whose ChromaDB vectors went missing after a crash between
# the two writes (otherwise the runbook is silently unretrievable). Two bounds
# cap the re-embed work, which runs on the startup path before readiness:
#   - KB_REPAIR_MAX_ROWS (default 25): above this many orphans the set is treated
#     as a bulk-loss anomaly (repair nothing + warn; recover with a full pack
#     re-ingest), not per-boot crash recovery.
#   - KB_REPAIR_MAX_CHUNKS (default 60): per-boot embedding budget; rows past it
#     defer to the next boot. Lower it if a tight k8s startupProbe leaves too
#     little time for the model load + re-embed.
# Both must be >= 1 (0/negative is rejected at startup). Takes effect on restart.
# KB_REPAIR_MAX_ROWS=25
# KB_REPAIR_MAX_CHUNKS=60

# Log level: DEBUG | INFO | WARNING | ERROR
# LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# API port (the process runner, scripts/faultmaven-dev.sh, reads this):
# PORT=8090

# Deployment environment: development | staging | production
#
# This is the whole gate on how hard the request protections bite. Rate limiting
# and request deduplication are installed from one of two presets, and this key
# is what picks between them:
#
#   absent, or `development` -> the PERMISSIVE preset. Roomy limits, and the
#                               bypass headers X-Dev-Bypass / X-Test-Bypass are
#                               LIVE: the mere PRESENCE of either header skips
#                               rate limiting entirely, no value required.
#   `production` (or         -> production's preset. Tight limits, no bypass
#   `staging`, which gets       headers, and fail-CLOSED on a Redis outage
#   the same preset)            (requests are refused rather than passed).
#   anything else            -> refuses to start (settings validation rejects
#                               unknown values before preset selection).
#
# Values are matched EXACTLY and are lowercase: `Production` is not `production`
# and is rejected at startup like any other unknown value.
#
# UNSET IS NOT STRICT — it reads as `development`. Never leave it unset, or set
# to `development`, on an internet-facing box: the bypass headers make the rate
# limiter opt-out for anyone who knows their names. Set `production` (or
# `staging`) on any deployed box.
#
# No value installs an empty protection stack; the choice is which preset, not
# whether. See docs/operations/security/client-protection.md.
# ENVIRONMENT=development

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. AUTH  (standalone = single-user local auth)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Standalone always uses local auth; OAuth/multi-user is a cloud capability. The
# OAuth and RS256 keys further down are documented for surface parity only —
# a standalone install leaves them commented, and a cloud deployment sets them
# through Kubernetes rather than here.
# AUTH_MODE=local
#
# The HS256 secret that signs your local session tokens is AUTO-GENERATED and
# persisted to data/.jwt_secret on first run — no action needed. To pin your own
# (e.g. to share tokens across machines), uncomment and set a long random value:
# JWT_SECRET_KEY=
#
# Token lifetimes. Each variable names its unit — they do NOT share one, and
# out-of-range values are rejected at startup.
#
# One pair, effective in BOTH auth modes — local/HS256 and cloud/RS256 mint from
# the same two values (#888). The older EXPIRE_* spelling is retired: setting
# either name now fails startup with an error naming its replacement.
# JWT_ACCESS_TOKEN_EXPIRY_MINUTES=15   # minutes, 1–1440   (both modes)
# JWT_REFRESH_TOKEN_EXPIRY_DAYS=7      # DAYS, 1–90        (both modes)
#
# RS256 key pair (cloud/OAuth only — standalone signs with JWT_SECRET_KEY above).
# Generate with: python scripts/generate_oauth_keys.py
# JWT_PRIVATE_KEY=
# JWT_PUBLIC_KEY=
#
# OAuth authorization-code flow (browser extension). The code lifetime is in
# SECONDS (60–1800); the default is the intended 10 minutes:
# OAUTH_CODE_EXPIRY_SECONDS=600
# OAUTH_REQUIRE_HTTPS_REDIRECT=true    # set false only for local http:// redirects
# OAUTH_REQUIRE_CONSENT=true
#
# The three list-valued OAuth knobs below are parsed as JSON, not as
# comma-separated text — a bare value fails at startup rather than being split.
#
# Where a code may be delivered. The default admits the launchWebAuthFlow
# redirect of ANY extension id, so unpacked dev builds work out of the box; the
# browser derives that host from the extension's own id, so a code still cannot
# reach an extension other than the one named in the URL. Pin your published id
# to narrow it to your build:
# OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI_PATTERNS=["^https://[a-p]{32}\\.chromiumapp\\.org/?$","^https://[a-f0-9]{40}\\.extensions\\.allizom\\.org/?$"]
#
# Which clients may skip the consent screen. BOTH are required, and only the
# redirect half proves anything — client_id is caller-supplied, so an impostor
# extension can claim `faultmaven-copilot` (and the consent screen never caught
# that: it renders the client NAME, so the impostor's prompt read "FaultMaven
# Copilot" too). The redirect patterns are EMPTY by default, which means every
# client gets the consent screen until you pin your published extension id:
# OAUTH_FIRST_PARTY_CLIENTS=["faultmaven-copilot"]
# OAUTH_FIRST_PARTY_REDIRECT_PATTERNS=["^https://<your-32-char-extension-id>\\.chromiumapp\\.org/?$"]
#
# Dashboard URL — acts as the IdP front end for the OAuth flow:
# DASHBOARD_URL=https://app.faultmaven.ai

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. CONTAINER IMAGES  (advanced — only relevant to ./faultmaven.sh)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ./faultmaven.sh start pulls pre-built images from GHCR. Tags default to
# :latest (auto-refreshed each start); pin for a reproducible, version-matched
# stack (a release version or an immutable sha-<short-sha>):
# FM_IMAGE_TAG=latest
# FM_DASHBOARD_IMAGE_TAG=latest
#
# To build from your own source instead: ./faultmaven.sh start --build (API) /
# --build-dashboard (Dashboard).

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. TIMEOUTS  (advanced — raise these for slow or reasoning-heavy models)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Seconds allowed for a single LLM call, and for a whole agent turn (which wraps
# the entire process_turn call and may make several). A reasoning model on a
# deep-context turn can exceed the defaults; a timeout surfaces as a failed turn,
# not a degraded answer. AGENT_REQUEST_TIMEOUT is range-checked at startup and an
# out-of-range value refuses the boot — raise it via the per-provider override
# below if you need longer than its ceiling.
#
# The two are not independent: a failing provider costs three attempts plus 14s
# of retry backoff, and one attempt costs whichever is LARGER of
# LLM_REQUEST_TIMEOUT and this provider's LLM_PROVIDER_TIMEOUT_OVERRIDES entry —
# so an override of 120 costs 374s per turn, not 104s. Raising either past what
# the turn allows buys fewer retries, not longer ones.
# GET /admin/config/status reports whether the pair fits, for the provider
# actually in use (llm_retry_ladder_fits_turn_budget).
# LLM_REQUEST_TIMEOUT=30                 # seconds, no enforced range
# AGENT_REQUEST_TIMEOUT=120              # seconds, 30–600
#
# Per-provider overrides, as a JSON object of provider -> seconds. Providers not
# listed use the timeout above:
# LLM_PROVIDER_TIMEOUT_OVERRIDES={"fireworks": 180}
# AGENT_PROVIDER_TIMEOUT_OVERRIDES={"fireworks": 300}

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. OBSERVABILITY  (advanced — Opik/Comet LLM tracing, unconfigured by default)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Opik traces every LLM call (prompt, response, latency, cost). FaultMaven
# configures a backend only when OPIK_ENABLED=true AND an endpoint is set —
# either OPIK_USE_LOCAL=true or OPIK_URL_OVERRIDE. The API key is NOT part of
# that gate; it is applied afterwards, so a wrong or missing key fails at send
# time rather than turning tracing off.
#
# Leaving OPIK_ENABLED false is fail-closed: every @opik.track call site
# re-reads this flag per call and runs the undecorated function, and startup
# flips the SDK's own kill switch (OPIK_TRACK_DISABLE + set_tracing_active
# (False)) so no Opik client is ever constructed and nothing calls out to the
# SDK's Comet Cloud default (#1121).
#
# Traces leave the machine when OPIK_URL_OVERRIDE points at hosted Comet — keep
# tracing off if prompts may carry customer data that has not been redacted.
# OPIK_ENABLED=false
# OPIK_API_KEY=
# OPIK_PROJECT_NAME=faultmaven
# COMET_WORKSPACE=
# OPIK_URL_OVERRIDE=                   # e.g. https://www.comet.com/opik/api
#
# SDK-level kill switch, read by the Opik SDK directly from the environment
# (not a settings.py field), so the default below is the SDK's. OPIK_ENABLED
# above already fails closed; set this true to force every @opik.track
# operation off even when OPIK_ENABLED=true:
# OPIK_TRACK_DISABLE=false

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. LIMITS & CORS  (advanced — defaults suit a local single-user install)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Browser origins allowed to call the API, as a JSON array. The dashboard origin
# and the extension schemes must stay listed or the UI cannot reach the API:
# CORS_ALLOW_ORIGINS=["http://localhost:3333","chrome-extension://*","moz-extension://*"]
# CORS_ALLOW_CREDENTIALS=true
#
# Evidence upload bounds:
# MAX_UPLOAD_SIZE_MB=10
#
# There is no rate-limit knob. The limits, windows and the on/off decision live
# in the two protection presets in faultmaven/config/protection.py and are
# chosen by ENVIRONMENT name. RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED and
# RATE_LIMIT_REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE were listed here and read by nothing; they are
# gone (fm#985). Two keys do reach the presets, and only these two --
# PROTECTION_RATE_LIMIT_FAIL_OPEN below and PROTECTION_TRUSTED_PROXIES.
#
# No key switches rate limiting off. SKIP_SERVICE_CHECKS used to, by skipping
# protection setup entirely, but it means "do not require external services" and
# the limiter requires none -- so it no longer gates the install (fm#990). The
# only way to end up serving without a limiter is protection setup raising on a
# deployment the development carve-out lets boot anyway; every other environment
# refuses to start. Check which state you are in with
# GET /api/v1/admin/config/status.
#
# When Redis is unreachable, pass requests unlimited (true) or refuse them with
# 503 (false). Read by the development preset only; the production preset --
# which every deployment other than ENVIRONMENT=development runs -- pins
# fail-closed regardless (its degraded rung is per-replica, so it is a floor
# rather than a substitute). Distinct from
# PROTECTION_FAIL_OPEN, which governs PII redaction:
# PROTECTION_RATE_LIMIT_FAIL_OPEN=true
#
# Proxies whose X-Forwarded-For header may be believed when deciding which
# client a rate limit applies to. Comma-separated addresses or CIDRs, written
# as network addresses (10.244.0.0/16, not 10.244.226.134/16 — a host with a
# mismatched prefix is rejected rather than widened).
#
# The default is EMPTY and that is correct for a standalone install: forwarding
# headers are then ignored entirely and limits key on the socket peer, so a
# caller cannot draw a fresh quota by rotating a header it controls. Set it
# ONLY to the address range of a proxy you actually run — behind a proxy,
# leaving it unset makes every client share one bucket (the server warns when
# it sees forwarding headers from an address that is not listed here):
# PROTECTION_TRUSTED_PROXIES=

# =============================================================================
# Notes
# =============================================================================
# • Fixed standalone backends (not set here): SQLite, in-process cache, embedded
#   ChromaDB, local file storage, local auth. Postgres/Redis/S3/multi-tenancy are
#   cloud-only and configured via Kubernetes, never in this file.
# • DEPLOYMENT_MODE defaults to 'standalone' — do NOT set 'cloud' here. Cloud is
#   driven by Kubernetes config, and the server fails fast on boot if it is told
#   'cloud' without the required cloud infrastructure (ADR-004 coherence gate).
